.TH LERNT 1 "2026-09-21" "lernt 2.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lernt \- drill a deck of Markdown flash cards
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lernt
.br
.B lernt
\fIDECK\fR [\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR] [\fB\-t\fR \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIFILTER\fR] [\fB\-w\fR | \fB\-l\fR] [\fB\-e\fR]
.br
.B lernt
\fIDECK\fR \fB\-\-misses\fR [\fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B lernt
\fIDECK\fR \fB\-\-boxes\fR
.br
.B lernt
\fIDECK\fR \fB\-\-edits\fR
.br
.B lernt \-\-list
.br
.B lernt \-\-new\-deck
\fINAME\fR [\fB\-\-about\fR \fITEXT\fR]
.br
.B lernt
\fIDECK\fR \fB\-\-new\fR \fIQUESTION\fR [\fB\-\-answer\fR \fITEXT\fR] [\fB\-\-follows\fR \fICARD\fR]... [\fB\-\-time\fR \fISECONDS\fR] [\fB\-\-as\-draft\fR]
.br
.B lernt\-deck
\fIDECK\fR [\fB\-\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-check\fR]
.br
.B lernt\-deck \-\-list
.br
.B lernt\-deck
\fB\-\-serve\fR | \fB\-\-schema\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lernt
asks a question from a deck, times your answer, reveals the card's answer,
takes a pass/shaky/fail score, and then asks where to go next \- one of the
follow-ups the card names, or somewhere random.
.PP
Every score also moves the card through a Leitner schedule \- see
.B LEITNER BOXES
below \- and
.B \-l
runs only the cards that schedule has due.
.PP
With no arguments at all,
.B lernt
starts the TUI that ships with it, when standard input and output are a
terminal. Where the TUI is not built, or there is no terminal, it lists the
decks instead. The TUI is handed the engine command in \fBLERNT_ENGINE\fR, a
JSON array, and reaches the engine through it and nothing else.
.PP
A
.I deck
is a directory holding a \fBdeck.toml\fR \- see
.B DECKS
below. There are no built-in paths and no built-in subject.
.PP
The tool is an engine with an API, and frontends over it. The engine reads a
deck's Markdown cards and decides everything about a run: which card comes
next, what is logged, what counts as a miss.
.B lernt
calls the API in-process; the TUI calls it over JSON-RPC, from
.BR "lernt\-deck \-\-serve" .
Writing a frontend means calling the same API \- see
.BR API .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS lernt
.TP
.I DECK
The deck to run: the bare name of a deck in the decks directory, or a path to a
deck directory (or its \fBdeck.toml\fR). A run option given without a deck is
an error listing the decks \- nothing is picked for you, because a run logs
against the deck it ran.
.TP
.BR \-n " " \fICOUNT\fR
Cards in the run. 0 runs until you stop it. Default 10.
.TP
.BR \-t " " \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds per answer for every card, before it is reported as over the limit, or
\fBoff\fR to run untimed. Without it, each card gets its own \fBTime\fR, and a
card with none gets the deck's \fBlimit\fR, else 30. The limit is advisory: it
never cuts an answer off.
.TP
.B \-a
Follow the branches automatically rather than offering a menu: an unasked
follow-up of the current card if there is one, otherwise a fresh random card.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-drafts
Let random jumps and
.B \-a
land on cards tagged \fB#draft\fR. They are excluded by default; a draft offered
as a follow-up on the menu is always reachable, and marked.
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIFILTER\fR
Restrict the pool to cards whose id contains \fIFILTER\fR, case-insensitively.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-weak
Drill only the cards on the miss list \- those whose most recent attempt was
shaky or fail.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-due
Drill only the cards the Leitner schedule has due today, new cards included,
lowest box first. Not with
.BR \-w .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-edit
Edit mode. A normal run with one more step after each score:
.RS
.TP
.B e
Open the card's file in \fBVISUAL\fR, else \fBEDITOR\fR, else \fBvi\fR, then
show it again as it now reads. An editor that saves nothing, or exits non-zero,
is not an edit.
.TP
.B n
Leave a note against the card.
.TP
.B x
Flag the card for removal, with an optional reason.
.TP
.B d
Clear what stands against the card without editing it here. Offered only when
something stands.
.TP
.B Enter
Go on.
.RE
.IP
Entries go to the edit log even under
.BR \-\-no\-log ,
which is about the run, not the deck.
.TP
.B \-\-edits
Print the edit list and stop: every card with notes or removal flags standing,
flags first, each with the path of its file.
.TP
.B \-\-boxes
Print how many cards sit in each Leitner box, how many of them are due today,
and, when none is, the day one will be. Then stop.
.TP
.B \-\-misses
Print the miss list and stop.
.B \-\-all
widens it to every card ever attempted.
.TP
.BR \-\-new\-deck " " \fINAME\fR
Make a new, empty deck in the decks directory and stop. Its id is made from
\fINAME\fR (\fBCapital cities\fR is \fBcapital\-cities\fR), its cards go beside
its \fBdeck.toml\fR, and a deck already there is never replaced.
.B \-\-about
gives it a description.
.TP
.BR \-\-new " " \fIQUESTION\fR
Add a card asking \fIQUESTION\fR to \fIDECK\fR, written into the deck's first
card directory. Its id is the question with what a filename cannot hold taken
out. Without
.B \-\-answer
the card is then opened in $VISUAL or $EDITOR to be written, and the deck is
checked when the editor closes (as
.B lernt\-deck \-\-check
does), listing anything wrong with it. Where there is no terminal the card is
left for you to open. A card that already has the id is never overwritten.
.TP
.BR \-\-answer " " \fITEXT\fR
With \fB\-\-new\fR, the card's answer.
.TP
.BR \-\-follows " " \fICARD\fR
With \fB\-\-new\fR, the id of a card the deck already has, for this one to lead
to. Repeatable. An id the deck does not have is refused and nothing is written.
.TP
.BR \-\-time " " \fISECONDS\fR
With \fB\-\-new\fR, the card's own time, or \fBoff\fR to answer it untimed.
.TP
.B \-\-as\-draft
With \fB\-\-new\fR, tag the card #draft.
.TP
.BR \-\-json " " \fIFILE\fR
Run from a deck JSON written by
.B lernt\-deck
instead of reading the cards.
.TP
.BR \-\-cards " " \fIDIR\fR
A card directory, repeatable. Runs with no deck at all; the run's id is then
\fBad-hoc\fR, and it logs under that.
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the decks, their banks and their logs, and stop. A deck that does not load
is listed as unusable rather than omitted.
.TP
.BR \-\-log " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-no\-log
Where attempts are recorded, or that they are not.
.SS lernt-deck
Takes the same \fIDECK\fR and \fB\-\-cards\fR as
.BR lernt .
.TP
.BR \-\-out " " \fIFILE\fR
Where to write the deck. Default stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report the card count, how many cards offer branches, how many are drafts, and
any follow-up pointing at a card that does not exist. Writes no deck. Exits 1 if
a problem was found.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Write every deck in the decks directory as JSON \- see
.BR "DECK LIST FORMAT" .
.TP
.B \-\-serve
Serve the API as JSON-RPC 2.0 on standard input and output, one message per
line, until standard input closes \- see
.BR API .
.TP
.B \-\-schema
Write every API method's params and result as JSON Schema.
.SH DECKS
A deck is a directory in the decks directory,
.IR ~/.local/share/lernt/decks ,
holding a \fBdeck.toml\fR. A symlink to a directory elsewhere \- a folder in a
notes vault \- is a deck too.
.PP
.nf
[deck]
name  = "Embedded screens"
description = "Questions from embedded technical screens"
cards = ["Questions"]
count = 10
limit = 30
boxes = [1, 2, 4, 8, 16]
.fi
.PP
Every key is optional, and an empty file is a deck of defaults.
.TP
.B name
What the deck is called on screen. Defaults to the directory name.
.TP
.B description
A line or two on what the deck is, shown beside it in the deck list.
.TP
.B cards
A directory, or a list of them, holding the cards. Defaults to the deck
directory itself. Relative paths resolve against the deck directory \- its real
location, when the deck is a symlink \- and absolute or \fB~\fR paths point
anywhere. Several directories are drilled as one pool, in the order written; if
two hold a card of the same id the first wins and
.B \-\-check
reports the clash.
.TP
.B count
The run's default card count, as
.BR \-n ,
which still overrides it.
.TP
.B limit
Seconds for a card that sets no \fBTime\fR of its own; 0 leaves such cards
untimed.
.B \-t
overrides it, along with every card's own time.
.TP
.B boxes
The Leitner schedule: a list of days, one per box, that a card in that box
waits between reviews. Defaults to \fB[1, 2, 4, 8, 16]\fR \- five boxes. Its
length is the number of boxes. Changing it re-reads every card's history under
the new schedule; nothing is stored to migrate.
.TP
.BR log ", " edits
Where the attempt and edit logs go, if not the defaults under
.BR FILES .
.PP
A deck's
.I id
is its directory name in the decks directory \- the link's name, for a symlink
\- and never its \fBname\fR. The id names the logs, so retitling a deck keeps
its history.
.SH CARD FORMAT
One Markdown file per card. The filename stem is the card's id, and it is the
identity everything else \- follow-up links, the logs \- refers to. Files whose
name starts with \fB!\fR are skipped.
.PP
.nf
# The question

> [!tip]
> The answer.

Notes, shown after the answer.

---

**Reference:** [[Some longer note]]
**Time:** 45s
**Follow-ups:**
1. [[Another card|Its question]]

#draft #other-tag
.fi
.PP
The first line starting \fB#\ \fR is the question. The first callout, of any
type, is the answer, and the rest of the body is notes; a card with no callout
is all answer. Everything below the first \fB---\fR is metadata: a
\fBReference\fR, a \fBTime\fR (\fB45\fR, \fB45s\fR, \fB2m\fR, \fB1m30s\fR, or \fBoff\fR to answer it untimed), numbered follow-up links \- the target's filename stem is the
id, and any folder path, heading or alias in the link is ignored \- and tags.
.PP
There is no "follows from" field. The reverse edges are computed, because a
stored copy of every edge is a second copy free to drift.
.SH DECK FORMAT
One JSON object,
.B schema
.BR lernt.deck/4 ,
with
.BR generated ,
.B deck
(the id),
.BR name ,
.B sources
(its card directories), a
.B cards
array and
.B problems
(dangling follow-ups and id clashes). Each card carries
.BR id ,
.B source
(the directory it came from),
.BR title ,
.B answer
and
.B notes
(arrays of Markdown lines),
.B followups
and
.B parents
(card ids),
.BR reference ,
.B time
(seconds; 0 untimed; null when the card sets none),
.B tags
and
.BR draft .
Card text is emitted as the Markdown it was written in; rendering it is the
frontend's job.
.SH DECK LIST FORMAT
.B lernt\-deck \-\-list
writes one JSON object,
.B schema
.BR lernt.decks/1 ,
with
.B home
(the decks directory) and a
.B decks
array. Each deck carries
.BR id ,
.BR name ,
.BR path ,
.B cards
(directories),
.BR count ,
.BR limit ,
.B log
and
.B edits
(its log paths). A deck that does not load carries
.BR id ,
.B path
and
.B error
instead.
.SH LOG FORMAT
Append-only JSON Lines, one file per deck. One object per attempt:
.B v
(format version),
.B at
(local ISO 8601),
.B run
(an id shared by one run's attempts),
.B card
(the id),
.B title
(as it read when asked),
.B score
(\fBpass\fR, \fBshaky\fR or \fBfail\fR),
.B seconds
and
.B reached
(\fBrandom\fR or \fBbranch\fR).
.PP
The log is keyed by card id and holds nothing positional, so adding cards or
reordering a deck cannot re-point a past result. A renamed card orphans its
own history and nothing else;
.B \-\-misses
names such cards as retired rather than dropping them.
.PP
A card is
.I on the miss list
when its most recent attempt was shaky or fail \- not when it has ever been
missed. One good answer takes it off.
.SH LEITNER BOXES
Each card sits in a box, and box \fIn\fR comes due the number of days the
deck's \fBboxes\fR gives for it after the card last moved. Boxes are not stored:
they are replayed from the attempt log, one attempt at a time, so they can never
disagree with it, and a history logged before the schedule existed counts.
.IP \(bu 2
A card never attempted is new, and due, as if it sat in box 1: a first pass
puts it in box 2, a first fail or shaky in box 1.
.IP \(bu 2
A pass on a card that is due moves it up a box, up to the last.
.IP \(bu 2
A pass on a card that is not due yet changes nothing, so drilling a card over
and over in one day does not rush it up the boxes.
.IP \(bu 2
A fail sends it back to box 1, due or not.
.IP \(bu 2
A shaky keeps it in its box and starts its wait again.
.PP
Every run moves cards, not only
.BR \-l :
a card failed anywhere has been forgotten. Days are calendar days in local time.
.SH EDIT LOG FORMAT
Append-only JSON Lines, one per deck, written by
.BR \-e .
One object per entry:
.BR v ,
.BR at ,
.BR run ,
.B card
and
.B title
as in the attempt log, then
.B kind
and
.BR text .
.B kind
is
.B note
(\fBtext\fR is the note),
.B remove
(\fBtext\fR is the reason, possibly empty),
.B edit
(the card file was changed from a run) or
.B done
(cleared by hand).
.PP
A note or removal flag
.I stands
until an
.B edit
or
.B done
for the same card follows it, or until the card is no longer in the deck.
.SH API
Each method takes one params object and returns one result object:
.B engine.info
and
.B decks.list
(each deck with its size, its cards by box, what is due and missed, and the
day it last ran);
.B run.start
(a deck and the run options; the first card),
.B run.score
(score and seconds),
.B run.menu
(the follow-ups),
.B run.next
(pick \fBauto\fR, \fBrandom\fR or \fBfollowup\fR) and
.BR run.end ;
.B card.edit
and
.B card.reload
(the file to open, then read it back),
.BR card.note ,
.B card.flag
and
.BR card.done ;
.BR report.misses ,
.B report.boxes
and
.BR report.edits ;
.B options.load
and
.B options.save
(a frontend's saved settings for a deck, as key and value strings; a save
merges, keeping keys it was not sent);
.B settings.load
and
.B settings.save
(the same, for lernt as a whole rather than one deck);
.B deck.create
and
.B card.create
(write a new deck, or a card into a deck's first card directory, refusing a name
or follow-up that would clash or dangle);
.B card.list
(a deck's card ids and questions, for choosing follow-ups) and
.B deck.check
(what is wrong with a deck's cards).
There is one run at a time, and the card methods act on the card it is on.
An error carries a code: 1 no such deck, 2 no cards matched, 3 nothing on the
miss list, 4 not at this point in the run, 5 invalid values, 6 nothing due
today, or one of
JSON-RPC's own. The full types are
.BR "lernt\-deck \-\-schema" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <package>/_bin/lernt-tui
The TUI, built from \fItui/\fR in the source tree by \fBmake\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lernt/decks/<id>/deck.toml
The decks. Honours \fBLERNT_DECKS\fR, then \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR. Symlinks count.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/lernt/<id>.jsonl
The attempt log, one per deck. Honours \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/lernt/<id>.edits.jsonl
The edit log, one per deck, beside it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/lernt/<id>.options.json
What the TUI's options screen was last set to for the deck: a JSON object of
option names to values. Delete it to go back to the deck's defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/lernt/settings.json
The TUI's settings for lernt as a whole, in the same form: \fBmode\fR
(\fBclassic\fR or \fBleitner\fR), and \fBcount\fR and \fBlimit\fR, the card
count and the seconds per answer that a deck starts from when its
.B deck.toml
sets none. They are changed on the TUI's settings screen, opened with
.B s
on the deck list, and read and written through the API as
.B settings.load
and
.BR settings.save .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LERNT_DECKS
The decks directory.
.TP
.B LERNT_ENGINE
Set by \fBlernt\fR for the TUI it starts: the command that runs
\fBlernt\-deck\fR, as a JSON array.
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Bases for the decks directory and the logs.
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
The editor
.B \-e
opens a card in. Split as a shell would, so \fBcode \-w\fR works.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
The score is yours. Nothing checks what you actually said.
.IP \(bu 2
The logs are local and single-machine. There is no sync.
.IP \(bu 2
Renaming a deck's directory starts its history over. Move its log files to the
new id if you want the history to follow.
.IP \(bu 2
Card ids are filenames, so renaming a card is a history-losing operation. It is
reported, never repaired.
